OFC360 Attendance frontend integration with Redux Toolkit
Implement the attendance integration in my existing OFC360 frontend. Edit the code and run the checks you can execute. Start from the current screens, navigation, authentication client and store. Preserve the app’s platform and UI. For React Native or Expo, use its existing native navigation, camera, permissions and app lifecycle. For a React web frontend, use its existing browser and SSR patterns. All employee records, shifts, attendance states, counts and charts shown to users must come from the backend.

This prompt includes the request contract extracted from apiofc360-dev(3).zip. It contains 54 distinct method/path pairs under /api/v1/attendance, 12 attendance-monitoring operations and 19 related routes, including compatibility aliases. Verify the current backend revision if available. Trace app/main.py, app/api/v1/router.py, app/api/v2/router.py, app/routers/attendance.py and app/attendance/routers/. Check the selected handler, its service and its request parser. For raw Request or dict handlers, OpenAPI can omit accepted fields. If only the frontend is available, implement from this contract, verify against an authorized local or staging backend where available, and name the checks that still need a running service.
Implement the employee flow
1. On opening attendance, load GET /api/v1/attendance/face-status and GET /api/v1/attendance/face/me. Show the employee name from data.employee.first_name and last_name when available. Handle a missing employee profile, no record today, permission denial and a failed request as distinct states. Refetch on foreground or screen focus and after mutations. Use the company business timezone for dates and overnight shifts.
2. For first enrollment, capture a fresh image after a user gesture and call POST /api/v1/attendance/face-enroll. On success refetch enrollment and today. Enrollment and punching are separate actions. For each punch or break endpoint that requires a capture, supply a fresh image. Offer replacement enrollment only through an explicit user action.
3. Use one punch API per action. The V1 biometric flow uses /face/check-in and /face/check-out. If the existing mobile flow uses the V2 PWA contract, keep both punches on that contract. Do not send both requests or fall back to the legacy core punch on an error. The V1 break endpoints and V1 enrollment remain separate calls. An optional geofence precheck displays the server’s verdict. Its HTTP success alone does not establish that the user is inside the boundary.
4. Drive the buttons from data.checked_in, data.checked_out, data.is_on_break and the returned active break. Display server times, durations and status. A rejected or timed-out request must not mark the employee present or checked out. Suppress rapid double taps. On an uncertain write or duplicate response, refetch today before presenting another action. V2 supports replaying the same logical request with its original payload and key. Generate a new key for a new action.
5. Open the camera only for the selected action. Stop tracks or release the native camera on capture completion, cancel, error, navigation, background or unmount. Cancel geolocation watches when done. Handle permissions being denied, GPS being unavailable, low accuracy and a face mismatch with actionable messages. Never send dummy coordinates, a cached photo, a client-computed face match or a hardcoded employee ID.
6. Wire personal history with server pagination. Use /face/history without employee_id for the employee. Use /face/team for direct reports and /face/company only for an authorized company view. Keep platform-owner screens separate from tenant attendance. Connect existing shift, roster, regularization, monitoring and export controls to the matching rows below. Give each existing action a real route or an explicit unavailable state with the missing backend capability in the handoff.
Implement the Redux layer
- Reuse the app’s Redux Toolkit store and RTK Query base API. If Redux is absent, add @reduxjs/toolkit and react-redux, one store and provider, the API reducer and middleware, and typed hooks where the project uses TypeScript. Extend the base API with injectEndpoints when available. Keep only UI selections in a normal slice. Page results, today’s state and lists belong in the RTK Query cache.
- Use the configured API origin or existing proxy. Preserve the version exactly once, including on V2 requests. Reuse the normal transport with Authorization: Bearer <access token>. Reuse the existing refresh flow, including its HttpOnly cookie and platform-specific credentials handling. Coalesce concurrent refreshes. Retry safe reads after a successful refresh and reconcile uncertain mutations before any retry. A 403 is a permission or business result, with its server message.
- Include dates, filters, pagination and applicable user/tenant scope in cache identity. Pause protected queries until session and tenant resolution completes. Clear the API cache and stale in-flight screen state on logout, tenant switch or user change. During SSR, scope the store to the request and initialize device APIs on the client.
- Keep raw images, base64, camera handles, precise capture coordinates, refresh tokens and private image URLs out of persisted state, logs, Redux DevTools and crash reporting. For face uploads use an in-memory capture transport with the shared auth client, then invalidate RTK Query tags. Alternatively dispatch an opaque short-lived capture ID and release its in-memory payload after the request. Avoid passing the raw capture in a thunk or RTK mutation argument visible to action loggers. Project response fields for the UI before caching sensitive records. Handle authenticated export bytes outside persisted Redux state.
- Build endpoint-specific types and response parsing. Support the success envelope’s data, nullable values, paginated data.items, binary downloads, and actual error shapes including code, message, errors[] and string/object/list detail. Preserve field-level 422 errors, handle 401/403/404/409/429 and network failures, and honor Retry-After when provided. Show an empty state only after a successful empty result.
