Backend details that affect the UI
- In the inspected router order, the core handlers serve GET /api/v1/attendance/analytics/rate, /analytics/trends and /analytics/weekly under that same attendance base. Use from_date / to_date for rate and trends, and weeks for weekly. The later face router registers these same paths with different filters. The distinct /analytics/distribution accepts start_date, end_date and view_mode. Confirm effective routing on the branch in use. For the calendar, pass the actual selected month and year since the snapshot has fixed defaults.
- /face/me returns booleans such as checked_in, checked_out, is_on_break and nested employee, shift, breaks, current_break and today_attendance. Use fields that actually serialize. The V1 face punch returns data.id. The V2 mobile punch uses data.attendance_id and data.idempotent_replay. Reread today after checkout, including when a break was active, since the server can close the break. The /face/history, /face/team and /face/company lists return data.items, total, page and limit.
- The core /team handler lists company records for today. For direct-report workflows use /face/team. The core /regularization GET lists by company. The employee may submit a correction via POST. Verify a self-scoped read before implementing “My requests.” The attendance router has no dedicated approve/reject regularization operation in this snapshot. The shift options route has an admin/manager gate, even though employees can submit a shift-change request. If the employee screen needs a selectable list, find an employee-authorized source on the current branch or identify that specific backend dependency.
- Snapshot V1 check-in accepts work_mode in its parser but resets it before geofence evaluation. GPS can be rejected even when a UI expects a remote exception. Show the server outcome. Keep disabled or unsupported controls explicit and report mismatches with handler evidence. For employee history or organization-wide metrics, require the correct backend data scope. Client filters cannot enforce authorization.
- Verify data provenance. The snapshot’s monthly /reports/attendance service returns fixed figures and /reports/export builds job metadata without queuing the job in that method. For downloads use the supported authenticated CSV or export route that returns the actual file. Keep the report unavailable if its figures cannot be obtained from an equivalent real source. The snapshot registers the same payroll cycle handlers through two mounts. For an existing payroll workflow prefer the single /api/v2/payroll/cycles/ prefix. The doubled /api/v2/payroll/payroll/cycles/ variants are redundant compatibility paths. Call one canonical route per action. These operations belong only to existing authorized payroll controls.
- Keep the attendance feature independent from legacy school routes and unrelated platform dashboards. If multiple endpoints feed the same screen, select the one whose data scope, filters and response match that screen. Implement a typed method for each supported attendance operation, and trigger a request only for a real user flow. Record a server dependency for each blocked operation.
Acceptance checks and final handoff
1. Use the frontend project’s package manager. Run its typecheck, lint, relevant tests and build. Add focused tests for URL building, required and optional fields, V1 nested versus V2 flat location, per-action idempotency, response mapping, auth/cache reset and mutation invalidation. Test fixtures belong in tests.
2. With local or staging test accounts, verify an employee can exercise first enrollment → check-in → start break → end break → checkout. Verify name, button states, timestamps, elapsed breaks and history after a refresh or app restart. Cover camera/GPS denial, no enrollment, low-quality image or mismatch, duplicate taps, a lost response and overnight attendance. Reconcile against the server on return from background. Keep raw biometric data out of evidence.
3. With the appropriate test roles, verify direct reports, company views, filters, corrections, schedules, shifts, roster create/bulk/edit, downloads and the existing monitor screens. Check that changing user or tenant clears old data, that employees stay in self-service and that a rejected or locked edit preserves the last saved record. Exercise deletions and payroll lock actions only on disposable test data when those workflows are part of the app.
4. Return a compact screen-to-endpoint map, the changed files, commands and outcomes, and any blocker with its route and field. Show which checks ran against a real backend, which used fixtures, and which need credentials or a device. Mark a workflow verified only after its normal UI action and server result have been observed.